misc: hy310-board-mgr: arm the fan-stall poweroff again

Patch 0141 removed the fan-stall shutdown together with the NTC one. For the
NTC that was right -- this unit has no thermistor (ntc_num = 0), and the curve
behind temp1_input is a vendor table for a board whose thermal design we never
verified. For the fan it was wrong, and Marco says so plainly (12.09.): the
tachometer is the protection this board actually has, and it is proven.

What 0141 argued -- "the driver cannot restart the fan, so powering off trades
a warning for an outage" -- is exactly backwards for a projector. The lamp and
the DLP keep producing heat whether or not the fan turns; an outage is the
cheap outcome. The vendor firmware calls kernel_power_off() after a prolonged
stall for the same reason.

Measured on this device (11.09.2026, doku/60 board-mgr): tacho by GPIO IRQ,
4860 RPM in normal operation; PB5 off -> 0 RPM; PB5 on -> 4770 RPM. So the
signal that triggers the shutdown is the one signal on this board that has
been switched on and off deliberately and answered correctly both times.

  - fan_stall_shutdown, bool, 0644, default on. Off restores the 0141
    behaviour (report only) without a rebuild -- do that before deliberately
    cutting the fan rail on a bench, or the measurement ends in a poweroff.
  - Trigger: rpm below fg-warn-speed (DT, default 1000) for fg-warn-cnt
    consecutive one-second samples (DT, default 8). The existing warning is
    kept and now precedes the shutdown.
  - orderly_poweroff(true), not kernel_power_off(): userspace gets its
    shutdown, and the force flag covers a wedged userspace. Requested once per
    boot (poweroff_started).
  - Only after system_state == SYSTEM_RUNNING (unchanged) and only for a fan
    whose tacho IRQ exists (unchanged) -- without a reading there is nothing
    to judge, and the driver still refuses to guess.

thermal_shutdown stays off. Nothing here reads temperature.

--- a/drivers/misc/hy310-board-mgr.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/hy310-board-mgr.c
@@ -133,6 +133,23 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(thermal_shutdown,
 		 "Power off when the NTC exceeds temp_poweroff (default: off, report only)");
 
+/*
+ * The fan is the only working heat protection this board has. It has no NTC
+ * (ntc_num = 0 in the vendor configuration), so temperature never becomes a
+ * trigger here -- but the tachometer does, and it is proven: PB5 off gives
+ * 0 RPM, PB5 on gives ~4800 RPM again (measured 11.09.2026). A projector whose
+ * fan has stopped must not keep running; the vendor firmware powers the device
+ * off on a prolonged stall for exactly that reason, and so do we.
+ *
+ * Disarm it (fan_stall_shutdown=0) before deliberately cutting the fan rail on
+ * a bench, otherwise the measurement ends in a poweroff after fg-warn-cnt
+ * seconds.
+ */
+static bool fan_stall_shutdown = true;
+module_param(fan_stall_shutdown, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(fan_stall_shutdown,
+		 "Power off when the fan stays below fg-warn-speed for fg-warn-cnt cycles (default: on)");
+
 /* Default configuration values */
 #define DEFAULT_PWM_PERIOD_NS		40000	/* 25 kHz */
 #define DEFAULT_TEMP_POWEROFF_C		60
@@ -242,6 +259,9 @@
 	int			 temp_poweroff_c;
 	int			 temp_warn_c;
 
+	/* Set once a poweroff has been requested, so it is requested only once */
+	bool			 poweroff_started;
+
 	/* Delayed work for fan RPM and NTC polling */
 	struct delayed_work	 fan_work;
 	struct delayed_work	 ntc_work;
@@ -400,9 +420,10 @@
 /**
  * hy310_fan_work_fn - periodic fan RPM measurement (runs every 1 second)
  *
- * Reads and resets the pulse counter, calculates RPM, checks against
- * the warn threshold.  If RPM is consistently below warn_speed, the
- * system is powered off (indicating a stalled fan in a hot projector).
+ * Reads and resets the pulse counter, calculates RPM, checks against the warn
+ * threshold. If the RPM stays below warn_speed for warn_cnt consecutive
+ * seconds, the system is powered off: a stalled fan in a projector is not a
+ * condition to keep running through (fan_stall_shutdown, default on).
  */
 static void hy310_fan_work_fn(struct work_struct *work)
 {
@@ -430,19 +451,29 @@
 			continue;
 
 		/*
-		 * A stall is reported, never acted on. The fan on this board is
-		 * an unregulated on/off load on the shared PB5 rail (see the
-		 * fan-power gpio-hog): the driver cannot restart it, so powering
-		 * the device off would trade a warning for an outage. The RPM is
-		 * in fan1_input; userspace decides what a stall means.
+		 * A stall is the one condition this board can detect and must
+		 * act on: the fan is an unregulated on/off load on the shared
+		 * PB5 rail, so the driver cannot restart it, and there is no
+		 * temperature sensor to fall back on. Warn at the threshold,
+		 * then hand over to orderly_poweroff() -- userspace gets its
+		 * shutdown, and the force flag makes sure a wedged userspace
+		 * does not keep a projector running with a dead fan.
 		 */
 		if (rpm < fan->rpm_warn_speed) {
 			fan->rpm_warn_cycles++;
-			if (fan->rpm_warn_cycles == fan->rpm_warn_cnt)
+			if (fan->rpm_warn_cycles == fan->rpm_warn_cnt) {
 				dev_warn(priv->dev,
 					 "fan%d: %u RPM below %u for %u cycles -- check the fan\n",
 					 i, rpm, fan->rpm_warn_speed,
 					 fan->rpm_warn_cnt);
+				if (fan_stall_shutdown && !priv->poweroff_started) {
+					priv->poweroff_started = true;
+					dev_emerg(priv->dev,
